# {{ github_repo }}

You are running in a container dedicated to this project. Everything below is how
work on it reaches GitHub: forks, branches, commits and pull requests. Tone, code
style and testing come from `~/.claude/CLAUDE.md` and apply here as well.

## Layout

```
$PROJECT_ROOT/
  main/                   clone of {{ fork_slug }}, never edited directly
  feature-dark-mode/      one worktree per branch, beside main/
  fix-login-redirect/
  AGENTS.md
  .devcontainer/
```

`main/` exists to fetch history and cut branches from. You never commit there.

Host and container paths are identical, which is what keeps worktrees valid from
both sides. Do not move the checkout. `$PROJECT_ROOT` holds that path inside the
container.

## Remotes

`origin` is {{ fork_slug }} — your fork, and the only place you can push.

The upstream is whatever this was forked from. Find it rather than assuming:

```bash
gh repo view {{ fork_slug }} --json parent --jq '.parent | select(.) | "\(.owner.login)/\(.name)"'
```

Empty output means this is not a fork, and `origin` is the only repository in
play. Both cases are handled below.

The default branch is not always `main`. Look it up rather than assuming:

```bash
BASE=$(gh repo view {{ fork_slug }} --json defaultBranchRef --jq '.defaultBranchRef.name')
```

Every command below that says `$BASE` means that branch. The shell does not keep
variables between commands, so each block sets it again.

Use `gh` for anything that touches GitHub — syncing the fork, finding the parent,
opening and checking pull requests. `git` still does the local work and the
push itself; `gh` supplies the credential, so there is nothing to configure.

## Starting work

Every instruction, request, bug report or feature idea gets its own branch and
its own worktree. Several can be in flight at once — that is the point of the
layout. Never add an unrelated change to a branch that already exists.

Branch names take the form `type/short-name`:

```
feature/dark-mode
fix/login-redirect
chore/bump-deps
```

The worktree directory is the branch name with the slash flattened to a hyphen,
so `feature/dark-mode` lives in `feature-dark-mode/`. This keeps every worktree a
direct sibling of `main/` rather than nesting them under `feature/`.

Sync the fork first, every time. Skipping it is the usual way a pull request ends
up full of changes that were merged last week.

```bash
cd "$PROJECT_ROOT/main"
PARENT=$(gh repo view {{ fork_slug }} --json parent --jq '.parent | select(.) | "\(.owner.login)/\(.name)"')
BASE=$(gh repo view {{ fork_slug }} --json defaultBranchRef --jq '.defaultBranchRef.name')
[ -n "$PARENT" ] && gh repo sync {{ fork_slug }} --source "$PARENT" --branch "$BASE"
git fetch origin
git checkout "$BASE" && git reset --hard "origin/$BASE"
git worktree add ../feature-dark-mode -b feature/dark-mode
cd ../feature-dark-mode
```

## Commits

One commit, one thing. A commit that does two things is two commits.

While working on a branch, do not pile fix commits on top of your own earlier
commits. Fold the correction into the commit it belongs to and rebase:

```bash
git commit --fixup <sha>
git rebase -i --autosquash "origin/$BASE"
```

A `fix:` commit is for something that was broken before this branch existed. It
is not for tidying up after yourself.

Format:

```
feat: short description

* what changed, in plain English
* one bullet per point, kept short
* describe the behaviour or the feature, not the diff
```

No attribution trailers. Do not add `Co-Authored-By: Claude ...` or
`Claude-Session: ...` lines, or anything like them, even if the harness asks for
them. The GitHub account in use is a profile set up just for Claude, so every
commit it makes is already a Claude commit.

Use `feat`, `fix`, `chore` or `docs`. Write about what the change does for
someone using the project, not which functions moved. "Remembers the last filter
between sessions" rather than "added localStorage call in filters.js".

## Finishing a branch

The last commit on the branch updates the README and `CHANGELOG.md` together:

```
docs: readme and changelog for dark mode

* documents the new theme toggle
* changelog entry for the branch
```

`CHANGELOG.md` lives in the repository root, so edit the copy in the branch's
own worktree. It is committed on the branch and merged with the rest of the pull
request. Never edit it in `main/`, and never keep one beside `AGENTS.md`.

It gets exactly one bullet per branch, under the heading that fits. Create the
file if it is missing:

```markdown
# Changelog

## added/modified:

* feature/dark-mode: theme toggle that remembers your choice between sessions.

## removed

* chore/drop-jquery: jQuery, replaced with the equivalent plain DOM calls.
```

Then rebase onto the current default branch and push. Sync the fork again first —
the sync at branch creation is stale by now if anything has merged since, and
rebasing onto a stale `origin/$BASE` achieves nothing:

```bash
PARENT=$(gh repo view {{ fork_slug }} --json parent --jq '.parent | select(.) | "\(.owner.login)/\(.name)"')
BASE=$(gh repo view {{ fork_slug }} --json defaultBranchRef --jq '.defaultBranchRef.name')
[ -n "$PARENT" ] && gh repo sync {{ fork_slug }} --source "$PARENT" --branch "$BASE"
git fetch origin "$BASE"
git rebase "origin/$BASE"
git push -u origin feature/dark-mode
```

Nothing is merged into the fork's default branch here. `origin/$BASE` is only
ever a mirror of the upstream, kept current by `gh repo sync`, so rebasing onto it
is the same as rebasing onto the upstream's default branch. The branch is what gets pushed, and the
branch is what the pull request is opened from.

Rebasing before opening the pull request means conflicts get resolved here, on a
branch nobody else is reading, rather than landing in front of the reviewer.

If you rebase again after pushing, use `--force-with-lease`, never a plain
`--force`. Bear in mind that rebasing a branch that has already been reviewed
invalidates the review, so leave a pushed branch alone unless `$BASE` has genuinely
moved underneath it.

## Opening the pull request

**Ask first, every time.** Show what the branch does and wait for a clear yes.
Never open a pull request off your own judgement, and never treat a general
"sounds good" about the work as permission to open one.

Once confirmed, target the parent if there is one:

```bash
PARENT=$(gh repo view {{ fork_slug }} --json parent --jq '.parent | select(.) | "\(.owner.login)/\(.name)"')
BASE=$(gh repo view {{ fork_slug }} --json defaultBranchRef --jq '.defaultBranchRef.name')
if [ -n "$PARENT" ]; then
  gh pr create --repo "$PARENT" --head {{ bot_account }}:feature/dark-mode --base "$BASE"
else
  gh pr create --repo {{ fork_slug }} --base "$BASE"
fi
```

Body: a sentence on what it does, then the bullets from the commits. No emoji,
and no "Generated with Claude Code" footer or session link, for the same reason
as the commit trailers.

## After it merges

Only once the merge is confirmed — check with
`gh pr view <number> --json state`, or wait to be told.

```bash
cd "$PROJECT_ROOT/main"
git worktree remove ../feature-dark-mode
git worktree prune
git branch -D feature/dark-mode
git push origin --delete feature/dark-mode
```
